    "artist": "Septfontaine",
    "credit": "Own work",
    "source": "Tassili Sahara 74",
    "description": "Location: On the southeastern edge of Tassili n'Ajjer, northwest of Djanet\nThe prevailing wind in the Sahara is the northeast trade wind (Harmattan). During the early Quaternary period, there were times when it blew much stronger than it does today. The sand in this picture was probably blown at that time from the Erg Ubari and Erg Murzouk areas in southwestern Libya across the gently ascending plateau of the Tassili n'Ajjer and deposited here, in the lee behind the mountain ridge.\n\nThe rounded boulders in the foreground are the result of insolation weathering. The large temperature differences between day and night build up tension in the rock, especially on protruding parts. These splinter off, and as the boulder becomes smaller and smaller, its shape becomes more and more rounded.",
    "dateCreated": "2011-11-11",
